SYSCALL REFERENCE: NETWORK AND SELECT

Socket wrappers exist in userland libraries for native C programs;
the tiny 'cc' compiler cannot call arbitrary socket functions.
The Python-like interpreter provides its own simplified socket API.

socket(domain/type...) / bind / listen / accept / connect
  Prepare TCP listeners/clients and UDP sockets. Exact native
  signatures are in userland/lib headers (do not guess Linux ABI).

send/recv, sendto/recvfrom
  Return byte counts or error. TCP is a byte stream; a single
  recv() need not return all sent data. UDP preserves datagrams.
  Close sockets after use. Use numeric IPv4 addresses as supported.

select(...)
  Wait for FD readiness; use it to multiplex several sockets on
  a single thread. Nonblocking reads may return immediately when
  no data is available. See userland examples for concrete ABI.

PYTHON SUBSET'S NETWORK API
  s = socket(2)
  sendto(s, "HI", "127.0.0.1", 2000)
  close(s)
  # UDP, type 2; TCP uses socket(1)
  # socket object is an integer descriptor, not CPython socket.

OBSERVE NETWORK STATE
  ip
  net
  net -a
  net -r
  cat /proc/net/wifi
  cat /proc/net/memory
  ping -c 2 127.0.0.1
  tcpdump -c 5

BOUND RECEIVE OPERATIONS
  UDP listeners, accept loops, recv loops and tcpdump without
  -c can block indefinitely. Start long-running services from
  interactive shell and send finite test requests via MCP.

SECURITY
  mcpd --shell exposes remote shell/file operations on TCP 8080
  without authentication or TLS. Use isolated trusted lab LAN.
